wow do I have a treat for you guys today  I'm holding an original guitar that's  over 180 years old this beautiful  instrument which was likely made in the  1840s was made by Salvador Ramirez in  Malaga Spain you can see it's like quite  the life and that has had many cracks  restored you can see it looks quite old  but still plays thanks to a lot of  people doing Restorations including  Richard Bernay whose shop I'm at now I'm  here with Marshall and Richard and  they've been kind enough to brief me  about this beautiful instrument in fact  I'm very lucky because today I'm  actually recording on this instrument  and those recordings will be part of an  exhibit at the national museum in South  Dakota it is so beautiful to look at and  actually the sound is amazing  thank you  thank you  like a lot of 19th century guitars at  the time the instrument is smaller and  it has quite a mellow sound it doesn't  have this sort of Canon effect that  modern guitars often go for modern  classical guitars that is they're going  for volume this is really not about  volume I think it's about charm  personally the sound is warm  and mellow and Beautiful on the body you  see this amazing Floral Design here  apparently this was done actually to  cover up an accident that the guitar had  at some point in its long life this  patch of wood here apparently is not  original it was replaced on the sound  board this whole thing is a different  piece of wood than the rest of the of  the top and apparently when this was was  done they then put on this beautiful  floral decided to sort of cover it up  the top itself is likely Spruce or fur  the back is made of Brazilian rosewood  which is an exotic rare wood and then  the strip here is this beautiful Bird's  Eye Maple a common wood for backs in the  19th century up here at the headstock  things get a little crazy we have number  one pegs which is characteristic of  early 19th century guitars these  friction pegs you just push in and turn  just like violins or cellos was used on  loots Baroque guitars it's actually very  common and they work quite well when  made well at both ends there's a cap of  ivory the actual design line of the of  the headstock here is sort of a scroll  effect which I have never seen before of  course I'm using gut strings which is  the strings that would have been on the  instrument at the time in fact before  1940 almost all guitars would have had  gut strings and God of course is  intestine usually sheep intestine it's  actually a fantastic sound super warm  mellow and you can feel the texture on  the on the strings if you don't know  about gut strings you can watch my video  all about it here let's listen to a  recording I made of a full piece for  this exhibition this is targa's famous  lagrama  thank you  thank you  foreign  piece but it's so beautiful and charming  and I think it really works with the  Mellow tone of this instrument now the  bridge is actually really interesting it  also has a bit of ivory on it some  inlays here just for beautiful  decoration and then the strings take a  weird path they actually go around the  bridge here going through the back end  then they pop out the the bridge side  underneath the strings which I've never  seen before Richard was telling me that  that's common for some Italian guitars  he's seen on the inside of the  instrument it's like sort of fascinating  world there's all these cleats which are  these wooden patches to sort of  um fix a crack and so there's really a  whole history in here of different  restoration attempts by different  luthiers now the instrument is smaller  than we're used to 19th century guitars  in general in the early 19th century  were were a bit smaller and that's part  of the reason they have the  characteristic sound that they do this  one has a surprisingly good bass range  for the size  foreign  so I'd highly recommend a visit to the  National Museum in South Dakota it's an  amazing Museum filled with loots Baroque  guitars and many other amazing  instruments and if you go at the right  time you might find this instrument with  my video playing next to it thanks so  much for watching if you like this video  please do subscribe my analytics tell me  that 70 of you who watch my videos  aren't subscribed so if you want to help  the channel grow please do like and  subscribe and you'll see my weekly  updates and before we go I want to thank  Richard Bernay for his great work on  this instrument and also recommended me  to record these pieces for the national  museum and also if you're interested in  classical guitar I have an online course  which is six hours long it's a beginner  course it is meant to take people along  their first about six months of learning  the classical guitar and I've heard from  so many of the students who've taken it  so far they seem to really love it and  can't wait for the intermediate course  which hopefully will be out very soon so  please check out the LinkedIn  description for classical guitar Pro and  until next time thank you guys for  watching  thank you
 

